    "wheat_plot_pair": {
      "name": "Wheat_Plot_Pair",
      "description": "Ad-hoc Phase-1 wheat plot as a 2-voxel mini-blueprint (round 49). ONE structural_fill is the mini-anchor the bot stands ON; ONE dirt is the crop cell it digs+places, tills and sows; 'growing' is external (the field subsystem owns the wheat above the dirt). No fence, no water bucket, no iron — the deliberately minimal Phase-1 payload. NOT sited by find_buildingspot's water-site overlay: the round-49 scanner (scenario_find_wheatplots) is the siting authority — it alone enforces hydration AND cross-plot penalty-free spacing, neither of which find_buildingspot knows — so this blueprint carries NO water voxel and is a pure BUILD payload. One instance = one plot; ~32 instances (each its own locked build_center, plus a quarter-turn rotation to aim the crop at the scanner's chosen cardinal) = one ad-hoc field. 'only care about the construction block being correct' → only the anchor cell must be valid land (requires_land); the dirt cell is dug+placed regardless. WHY THE ANCHOR IS 'stand_ground' AND EXTERNAL (Architect 2026-07-21): it used to be 'structural_fill', which made it a BUILD payload — and sand and gravel are deliberately NOT members of that group (they fall, so they must never satisfy a wall), so a plot sited on a sandy bank classified its own anchor as a group_mismatch and dug the sand out to place PLANKS in it. The anchor is not a structure; it is whatever ground the scanner already proved standable at siting time. External means the survey never places it and NEVER DIGS IT — 'its not supposed to mine anchors like gravel or sand. just the plot block.' Its own type name rather than adding 'structural_fill' to the external list, so a future genuine structural voxel added here is not silently ignored. The trade, stated: a vanished anchor is no longer repaired by the structural pass — acceptable because resolveStandableAnchor already relocates the bot to a standable lip at runtime.",
